parts.solution:
Sketch \(y = x + \sin x\) for \(-2\pi \le x \le 2\pi\).

Features from earlier parts:
• \(y^{prime} = 1 + \cos x \ge 0\), so the curve is non-decreasing;
• horizontal points of inflection at \((-\pi,-\pi)\) and \((\pi,\pi)\);
• inflection at the origin;
• the curve oscillates gently about the line \(y = x\) with amplitude 1.

Draw a smooth increasing curve about the dashed line \(y = x\), marking the POI.

Sketch \(y = x - \cos x\) for \(-2\pi \le x \le 2\pi\).

Features:
• \(y^{prime} = 1 + \sin x \ge 0\), so non-decreasing overall;
• the curve rides about the line \(y = x\) as \(-\cos x\) oscillates;
• mark horizontal points of inflection consistent with earlier parts (where \(y^{prime} = 0\) and \(y^{primeprime} = 0\)).

Draw a smooth non-decreasing curve about dashed \(y = x\) with the correct POI marked.

Rewrite \(y = \cos x + \sqrt{3}\sin x = 2\sin\!\left(x + \dfrac{\pi}{6}\right)\) (amplitude 2).

On \([0, 2\pi]\):
• maximum at \(x = \dfrac{\pi}{3}\), \(y = 2\);
• minimum at \(x = \dfrac{4\pi}{3}\), \(y = -2\);
• smooth sine wave of period \(2\pi\) and amplitude 2.

Sketch one full wave on the interval with these stationary points marked. Do not use amplitude \(1+\sqrt{3}\).

